    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Waverly was founded in 1878, establishing itself as a new community in Coffey County, Kansas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waverly%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Named after Waverly, Indiana, by a pioneer settler, the town carries a legacy from its namesake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waverly%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1877, the Ottawa & Burlington Railroad began, and Waverly became a key junction of the Atchison, Topeka & Santa Fe and Missouri Pacific lines.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/waverly-kansas/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1895, the Day Star Daughters founded Waverly’s first free public library, sparked by their teacher, M.L.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/waverly-kansas/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By the early 1900s, Waverly boasted waterworks, electric lights, banks, and a newspaper, marking it as a modern town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/waverly-kansas/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Waverly’s first post office opened on June 10, 1878, serving the growing community.",
